/{id} — full update, 200, validate title & author required, 404 if missing, 400 if invalid\n- DELETE /books/{id} — 204, 404 if missing\n- GET /health — 200 {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n\nStructure:\n- `app.py` — Flask application factory + routes\n- `db.py` — SQLite connection handling\n- `test_app.py` — tests using Flask test client\n- `requirements.txt`\n- `README.md`\n\nFor SQLite, use `sqlite3.Row` for dict-like access. Use per-request connection with teardown, or thread-local. Flask with the dev server is single-threaded by default but let's be safe and open a connection per request via g.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\napp.py:\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\nfrom flask import Flask, g, jsonify, request\n\nDATABASE = \"books.db\"\n\n\ndef create_app(database=DATABASE):\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n    app.config[\"DATABASE\"] = database\n\n    def get_db():\n        if \"db\" not in g:\n            g.db = sqlite3.connect(app.config[\"DATABASE\"])\n            g.db.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n            g.db.execute(\"PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON\")\n        return g.db\n\n    ...\n```\n\nActually, I'll make db.py contain the connection helpers and schema init so it's clean. Let me keep it reasonably simple: one `app.py` with app factory, `db.py` with database functions. Or a package? The task says \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — flat files in the current dir are fine.\n\nLet me write:\n\ndb.py:\n```python\n\"\"\"SQLite persistence for the books API.\"\"\"\nimport sqlite3\n\nSCHEMA = \"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    title TEXT NOT NULL,\n    author TEXT NOT NULL,\n    year INTEGER,\n    isbn TEXT\n);\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef connect(path):\n    conn = sqlite3.connect(path)\n    conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n    return conn\n\n\ndef init_db(path):\n    conn = connect(path)\n    try:\n        conn.executescript(SCHEMA)\n        conn.commit()\n    finally:\n        conn.close()\n```\n\napp.py — Flask app:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"REST API for managing a book collection.\"\"\"\nimport sqlite3\n\nfrom flask import Flask, g, jsonify, request\n\nfrom db import connect, init_db\n\nBOOK_FIELDS = (\"title\", \"author\", \"year\", \"isbn\")\nREQUIRED_FIELDS = (\"title\", \"author\")\n\n\ndef create_app(database_path=\"books.db\"):\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n    app.config[\"DATABASE\"] = database_path\n\n    with app.app_context():\n        init_db(app.config[\"DATABASE\"])\n\n    def db():\n        if \"db\" not in g:\n            g.db = connect(app.config[\"DATABASE\"])\n        return g.db\n\n    @app.teardown_appcontext\n    def close_db(exc):\n        conn = g.pop(\"db\", None)\n        if conn is not None:\n            conn.close()\n\n    @app.get(\"/health\")\n    def health():\n        return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\n    ...\n```\n\nWait — there's a subtlety with `g.pop` in teardown. Actually `g.pop` works. But if \"db\" not in g, pop returns None. Fine.\n\nValidation function:\n\n```python\ndef validate_book(data, require_all):\n    ...\n```\n\nRules:\n- Body must be a JSON object → 400\n- title and author must be present and non-empty strings → 400\n- year: if present, must be an integer (bool excluded) → 400\n- isbn: if present, must be a string → 400\n- Unknown fields: ignore or 400? I'll ignore them for simplicity... Actually being strict is nicer but ignoring is more common. I'll ignore unknown fields.\n\nFor PUT: the task says \"Update a book\". Common interpretation: full replacement requiring title/author, or partial update. I'll implement PUT as full update (title and author required per validation rule \"title and author are required\"), which matches the constraint. Hmm, but some might expect PATCH-like behavior. PUT semantics = replace, so requiring all required fields is correct. I'll document it.\n\nRoutes:\n\n```python\n@app.post(\"/books\")\ndef create_book():\n    data = request.get_json(silent=True)\n    error = validate(data, require_all=True)\n    if error:\n        return error\n    conn = db()\n    cur = conn.execute(\n        \"INSERT INTO books (title, author, year, isbn) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n        (data[\"title\"], data[\"author\"], data.get(\"year\"), data.get(\"isbn\")),\n    )\n    conn.commit()\n    book = get_book_row(cur.lastrowid)\n    return jsonify(row_to_dict(book)), 201\n```\n\nGET /books with ?author= filter:\n```python\n@app.get(\"/books\")\ndef list_books():\n    author = request.args.get(\"author\")\n    query = \"SELECT * FROM books\"\n    params = ()\n    if author is not None:\n        query += \" WHERE author LIKE ? COLLATE NOCASE\"\n        params = (f\"%{author}%\",)\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, `COLLATE NOCASE` placement: `WHERE author LIKE ? COLLATE NOCASE` — the collate applies to the LIKE comparison. That's valid SQLite syntax. Alternatively `LIKE ?` with lower(). Since author names, case-insensitive substring seems good. Let me simplify: `WHERE lower(author) LIKE lower(?)` with `%{author}%`.\n\nGET /books/<int:book_id> — 404 handling:\n```python\n@app.get(\"/books/<int:book_id>\")\ndef get_book(book_id):\n    row = db().execute(\"SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)).fetchone()\n    if row is None:\n        return jsonify({\"error\": f\"Book {book_id} not found\"}), 404\n    return jsonify(row_to_dict(row))\n```\n\nPUT /books/<int:book_id>:\n```python\n@app.put(\"/books/<int:book_id>\")\ndef update_book(book_id):\n    # check exists first\n    ...\n```\n\nDELETE:\n```python\n@app.delete(\"/books/<int:book_id>\")\ndef delete_book(book_id):\n    cur = db().execute(\"DELETE FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,))\n    conn.commit()\n    if cur.rowcount == 0:\n        return 404\n    return \"\", 204\n```\n\nError handlers for 404/405 to return JSON? Nice touch:\n```python\n@app.errorhandler(404)\ndef not_found(e): return jsonify({\"error\": \"Not found\"}), 404\n```\n\nAlso a JSON error handler for bad int conversion — Flask returns 404 for `<int:book_id>` mismatch automatically (e.g. /books/abc → 404). Fine.\n\nAlso add `if __name__ == \"__main__\": app.run(debug=True)` — use debug=False? For dev, `app.run(host=\"0.0.0.0\", port=5000)`. README will mention `flask --app app run` or `python app.py`.\n\nMain module-level app creation: `app = create_app()` so `flask --app app run` works, and tests import `create_app`. But module-level creation triggers init_db on import — that's fine, creates books.db in cwd. For tests I'll use tmp_path database to avoid clobbering.\n\nWait, one issue: `with app.app_context(): init_db(...)` inside create_app — creating the schema at app creation. Good.\n\nThreading: sqlite3 connections per request via g; default sqlite3.connect has check_same_thread=True but since each request creates and uses its connection in the same thread, that's fine.\n\nValidation details:\n- `year` bool check: `isinstance(year, bool)` is subclass of int, so exclude bools.\n- year as string \"2020\"? Reject — must be int. Or accept and coerce? I'll reject with 400 and clear message. Keep it strict and documented.\n- Negative years? Allow (BCE?). Fine, allow any int.\n\nResponse format for errors: `{\"error\": \"...\"}`.\n\nAlso 415 when Content-Type isn't JSON? `request.get_json(silent=True)` returns None → my validation says \"Request body must be a JSON object\" → 400. Good enough; maybe distinguish. If content type isn't application/json and body present... silent=True handles it. I'll return 400 with a helpful message. Some tests check 415, but 400 is acceptable given \"appropriate status codes\".\n\nTests (pytest, using tmp_path db and Flask test client):\n\n1. test_health — 200, {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n2. test_create_book — POST valid → 201, location? and returned fields; verify GET /books/{id}\n3. test_create_book_missing_title → 400\n4. test_create_book_missing_author → 400\n5. test_create_book_invalid_year → 400\n6. test_list_books / filter by author\n7. test_get_book_not_found → 404\n8. test_update_book — PUT → 200 fields